lus 9 (from the thousands place of 229,230). Wait, hold on. Wait, maybe I need to check the digits again.\n\nWait, actually, let me write both numbers digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 9 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 2 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nWait, so let me make sure each place is aligned properly:.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 9 and 2.\n- Ten thousands: 9 and 2.\n- Thousands: 7 and 9.\n- Hundreds: 8 and 2.\n- Tens: 7 and 3.\n- Ones: 1 and 0.\n\nSo, adding each column:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\nOnes: 1 + 0 = 1.\n\nTens: 7 + 3 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 8 + 2 = 10, plus 1 carried over is 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 7 + 9 = 16, plus 1 carried over is 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 9 + 2 = 11, plus 1 carried over is 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 9 + 2 = 11, plus 1 carried over is 12. So, write 2 and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, that 1 is added as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting all together, the digits from right to left are:.\n\n1 (ones), 0 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 7 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 2 (hundred thousands), and the carried-over 1 in the millions place.\n\nTherefore, the total is 1 (million) 2 (hundred thousands) 2 (ten thousands) 7 (thousands) 1 (hundred) 0 (tens) 1 (ones). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
