rry over 1 to the hundred-thousands place. .\n\nWait, but then the hundred-thousands place: first number has 2, second has 3. So 2 +3 =5, plus the carried-over 1 makes 6. .\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 12 → 2, carry 1.\nTens: 12 → 2, carry 1.\nHundreds: 9 (no carry).\nThousands: 6 (no carry).\nTen-thousands: 11 → 1, carry 1.\nHundred-thousands: 6.\n\nWait, but let me write out the total step by step. Let me reconstruct the sum step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 3 + 9 = 12 → write 2, carryover 1.\nTens place: 2 + 9 = 11, plus 1 = 12 → write 2, carryover 1.\nHundreds place: 2 + 6 = 8, plus 1 = 9 → write 9, no carryover.\nThousands place: 5 + 1 = 6 → write 6.\nTen-thousands place: 4 +7 = 11 → write 1, carryover 1.\nHundred-thousands place: 2 +3 =5, plus 1 =6 → write 6.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from right to left: 2 (ones), 2 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 1 (ten-thousands), 6 (hundred-thousands). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
