red thousands).\n\nWait, hold on, I think I might have messed up the hundreds place for the second number. Let me check again. The second number is 136,202. Breaking it down:.\n\n- 1 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 6 (thousands).\n- 2 (hundreds).\n- 0 (tens).\n- 2 (ones).\n\nYes, that's correct. So the hundreds digit is 2, not 6. Wait, thousands place is 6, hundreds is 2. Got it.\n\nSo going back to adding. Let me try again step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 7 + 2 = 9. Write down 9.\n\nTens place: 3 + 0 = 3. Write down 3.\n\nHundreds place: 7 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 9. Write down 9.\n\nThousands place: 4 (first) + 6 (second) = 10. So write down 0 and carry over 1 to the next (ten thousands) place.\n\nTen thousands place: 9 (first) + 3 (second) + 1 (carryover) = 13. So write down 3 and carry over 1 to the hundred thousands place.\n\nHundred thousands place: 9 (first) + 1 (second) + 1 (carryover) = 11. So write down 11. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
