undreds:8.\n\n4: Thousands:9.\n\n5: Ten-thousands:0 (since we had 10 here, so 0 with a carryover).\n\n6: Hundred-thousands:2 (from 2+9+1=12, so 2 here and carryover 1).\n\n7: Millions:1 (from the carryover).\n\nSo putting it all together from left to right:.\n\nMillions place:1.\n\nHundred-thousands:2.\n\nTen-thousands:1 (Wait, no, the ten-thousands place was 0, but where did the 1 come from?).\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me think again. Let's list each digit's place step by step.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):.\n\n- Units place: 3 +7 =10 →0, carryover 1.\n\n- Tens place:1 +5 +1=7 →7, carryover 0.\n\n- Hundreds place:4 +4=8 →8, carryover 0.\n\n- Thousands place:8 +1=9 →9, carryover 0.\n\n- Ten-thousands place:4 (from first number) +6 (second number) =10 →0 with carryover 1.\n\n- Hundred-thousands place:2 +9 +1 (carryover)=12 →2 in hundred thousands, carryover 1.\n\n- The carryover 1 goes to the millions place, which is a new digit at the front.\n\nSo the final number is 1 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 1 (ten thousand?), wait no:.\n\nWait, the places are:.\n\nMillions, Hundred-thousands, Ten-thousands, Thousands, Hundreds, Tens, Ones.\n\nWait, actually, the places are:.\n\n1st digit (leftmost): Millions.\n\nThen Hundred thousands, Ten thousands, Thousands, Hundreds, Tens, Ones.\n\nWait, but since the original numbers are only up to hundred thousands, adding them could result in a 7-digit number.\n\nSo the total digits would be:.\n\nMillions place:1.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands: (the 5th digit from the right is ten thousands, which was 0 from the ten-thousands place calculation?).\n\nWait, let me list all digits:.\n\nThe result digits from the addition are:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones:0.\n\n2. Tens:7.\n\n3. Hundreds:8.\n\n4. Thousands:9.\n\n5. Ten-thousands:0 (since 4+6=10, so 0 with carryover 1).\n\n6. Hundred-thousands:2 (from 2+9 +1=12, so 2 here).\n\n7. Millions:1 (from the carryover).\n\nSo arranging from