,000 = 789,411\n\n789,411 + 2,000 = 791,411\n\n791,411 + 400 = 791,811\n\n791,811 + 60 = 791,871\n\n791,871 +6 = 791,877.\n\nSame result. Hmm. So I think that's correct. Maybe I was confused when I thought I might have made a mistake, but after cross-verifying, it seems right. So the sum of 559,411 and 232,466 is 791,877.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe using another method: calculator-style addition.\n\nLet me write it vertically again:\n\n\u202f\u00a0559411\n\n+232466\n\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes digit: 1 +6 =7\n\nTens:1+6=7\n\nHundreds:4+4=8\n\nThousands:9+2=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands:5+3=8, plus carryover 1 is 9.\n\nHundred-thousands:5+2=7.\n\nSo the total is 791,877. Yep. Okay, so I think that's correct. Therefore, the answer is 791,877.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe sum of 559411 and 232466 is \\boxed{791877}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the sum of 559,411 and 232,466, we start by aligning the numbers by their place values:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 559411 \\\\\n+232466 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n1. **Ones place**: \\(1 + 6 = 7\\)\n2. **Tens place**: \\(1 + 6 = 7\\)\n3. **Hundreds place**: \\(4 + 4 = 8\\)\n4. **Thousands place**: \\(9 + 2 = 11\\) (write down 1, carry over 1)\n5. **Ten thousands place**: \\(5 + 3 + 1 = 9\\) (including the carryover)\n6. **Hundred thousands place**: \\(5 + 2 = 7\\)\n\nPutting all the digits together, we get the result:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 559411 \\\\\n+232466 \\\\\n\\hline\n 791877 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of 559,411 and 232,466 is \\(\\boxed{791877}\\).",
