rried over 1. 8 + 2 is 10, plus 1 is 11. So that would be 11 in the hundred thousands place? Wait, but that would mean we have an extra digit. Wait, actually, the hundred thousands place is the sixth digit from the right. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me write each number with commas to see:.\n\n853,753 is 8 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\n268,010 is 2 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (ten), 0 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 3 + 0 = 3.\n\nTens: 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nHundreds: 7 + 0 =7.\n\nThousands: 3 + 8 =11. So write down 1 and carry over 1.\n\nTen thousands: 5 + 6 =11, plus the carried over 1 is 12. So write 2 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 8 +2 =10, plus the carried over 1 is 11. So here, since we are in the hundred thousands place, adding 8 + 2 gives 10, plus 1 is 11. So that would be 11 in the hundred thousands place. But how do we write that? That would mean we have 11 in the hundred thousands place, which is actually 1,100,000. So the total would be 1,1 (hundred thousands) but that's not a digit. So actually, the 11 in the hundred thousands place would mean we have to carry over again to the millions place. .\n\nAh, right! Because 8 (hundred thousands) plus 2 (hundred thousands) is 10 hundred thousands, which is 1,000,000. Then adding the carried over 1 gives 1,000,000 + 100,000? Wait, maybe I need to think in terms of digits.\n\nWait, let me try to write it out step by step with the columns:.\n\nLet me write each number digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 8 5 3 7 5 3.\n\nSecond number: 2 6 8 0 1 0.\n\nWait, but actually, the first number is 8 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 3 (ones). The second number is 2 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (ten), 0 (ones). So when adding, each digit is in the same place:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):.\n\n3 + 0 = 3.\n\nNext (tens): 5 + 1 = 6.\n\nNext (hundreds): 7 + 0 =7.\n\nNext (thousands): 3 + 8 =11. So write down 1, carryover 1.\n\nNext (ten thousands): 5 +6 =11, plus carryover 1 is 12. So write 2, carryover 1.\n\nNext (hundred thousands): 8 +2 =10, plus carryover 1 is 11. So here, since this is the hundred thousands place, 11 can't be a single digit. So we have to carryover again. 11 in the hundred thousands place means that's 1,100,000. But since each digit can only go up to 9, we need to carryover to the millions place. So the 11 becomes 1 in the millions place and 1 in the hundred thousands place? Wait, no, actually, when you have 11 in the hundred thousands place, you can think of it as 1,000,000 + 100,000. So the total would be 1 (million) and 1 (hundred thousand). Therefore, the result would have a 1 in the millions place, 1 in the hundred thousands, then the digits from the previous carries. .\n\nWait, let me structure this properly. Let me go through each column again with the carries:.\n\nLet me represent each digit position with the addition and carryover:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones place: 3 + 0 = 3. Write 3. No carryover.\n2. Tens place: 5 + 1 = 6. Write 6. No carryover.\n3. Hundreds place: 7 + 0 = 7. Write 7. No carryover.\n4. Thousands place: 3 + 8 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n5. Ten thousands place: 5 + 6 = 11 + 1 (carryover) = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n6. Hundred thousands place: 8 + 2 = 10 + 1 (carryover) = 11. Here, since this is the hundred thousands place, 11 means 1,100,000. So we write down 1 in the hundred thousands place and carryover 1 to the millions place.\n7. Millions place: The carryover 1 from the previous step. Since there are no more digits to add here, we just write that 1.\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, the digits are:.\n\nOnes: 3.\n\nTens: 6.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nS