39525}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the sum of 664,196 and 575,329, we start by aligning the numbers by their place values and adding each column from right to left:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  664196 \\\\\n+ 575329 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n- Ones place: \\(6 + 9 = 15\\). Write down 5, carry over 1.\n- Tens place: \\(9 + 2 + 1 = 12\\). Write down 2, carry over 1.\n- Hundreds place: \\(1 + 3 + 1 = 5\\). Write down 5.\n- Thousands place: \\(4 + 5 = 9\\). Write down 9.\n- Ten-thousands place: \\(6 + 7 = 13\\). Write down 3, carry over 1.\n- Hundred-thousands place: \\(6 + 5 + 1 = 12\\). Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nThe carried over 1 from the hundred-thousands place becomes a new digit in the millions place. Combining all these, we get:\n\n\\[\n1,239,525\n\\]\n\nVerification by breaking the numbers into parts:\n- \\(664,196 + 575,329 = (600,000 + 64,196) + (500,000 + 75,329)\\)\n- Adding the large parts: \\(600,000 + 500,000 = 1,100,000\\)\n- Adding the smaller parts: \\(64,196 + 75,329 = 139,525\\)\n- Total: \\(1,100,000 + 139,525 = 1,239,525\\)\n\nApproximation check:\n- Approximating 664,196 to 664,000 and 575,329 to 575,000 gives \\(664,000 + 575,000 = 1,239,000\\). The exact sum is 1,239,525, which is 525 more, matching the rounded values' difference.\n\nThus, the sum of 664,196 and 575,329 is \\(\\boxed{1239525}\\).",
