n\nPutting it all together: 1 (from the final carryover) 0 (hundred thousands), then 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait, let me check again step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the thousands place. Let me go through each column again carefully.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nOnes place:.\n5 + 6 = 11. Write down 1, carryover 1.\n\nTens place:.\n1 (from first number) + 0 (from second) + 1 (carryover) = 2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place:.\n7 (first) + 4 (second) = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:.\n9 (first) + 4 (second) = 13. Plus the carried 1 makes 14. So write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place:.\n7 (first) + 4 (second) = 11. Plus 1 carried over is 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place:.\n6 (first) + 3 (second) = 9. Plus 1 carried over is 10. So we write 0 here and carryover 1. Since there are no more digits, that 1 is added as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from right to left: the ones digit is 1, tens 2, hundreds 1, thousands 4, ten thousands 2, hundred thousands 0, and then the final carried 1. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
