), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 8 (ones). So when adding, each column is correct.\n\nStarting over, let me do each step carefully:.\n\nOnes place: 2 + 8 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 5 + 5 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 4 + 4 = 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place: 5 + 9 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 8 + 8 = 16 + 1 = 17. So here, the 17 is the total for the hundred thousands and higher. Wait, actually, the hundred thousands place can only be a single digit, so 17 would mean 7 in the hundred thousands and carryover 1 to the millions place. But since both original numbers are six digits, adding them can result in a seven-digit number.\n\nAh, right! I forgot that adding two six-digit numbers can give a seven-digit number. So actually, the 17 in the hundred thousands place should be split into 7 in the hundred thousands and a carryover of 1 to the millions place. So the final digits would be:.\n\nMillions place: 1 (carried over).\n\nHundred thousands: 7.\n\nTen thousands: 4.\n\nThousands: 9.\n\nHundreds: 1.\n\nTens: 1.\n\nOnes: 0. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
