o the millions place. But since we don't have a millions place in the original numbers, that carryover becomes a new digit.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:1.\n\nTens:8.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:2 (with a carryover of 1).\n\nHundred thousands: 9+4=13 plus the carried 1 is 14? Wait, hold on. Let me recast this properly.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me do it step by step again.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically aligned:.\n\n\u2003 9 5 2 8 6 0.\n\n+ 4 7 4 1 2 1.\n\n------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\nOnes place: 0 +1 =1. Write down 1.\n\nTens place:6 +2=8. Write down 8.\n\nHundreds place:8 +1=9. Write down 9.\n\nThousands place:2 +4=6. Write down 6.\n\nTen thousands place:5 +7=12. Here, we write down 2 and carry over 1 to the next column (hundred thousands).\n\nHundred thousands place:9 +4=13, plus the carried over 1 makes 14. So we write down 4 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place.\n\nSince there are no more digits, we add that carried over 1 as a new digit in the millions place. So the total becomes 14 (hundred thousands) plus the carried over 1 million, making it 1,4 (hundred thousands). Wait, actually, the way it works is:.\n\nThe hundred thousands column after adding 9 and 4 plus the carryover gives 14. Since 14 in the hundred thousands place would actually be 1,400,000. But since we can't have two digits in a single place, we carry over the '1' to the millions place. Therefore, the total is 1 (million) 4 (hundred thousands), and the rest of the digits as calculated.\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum is:.\n\n1 (million) 4 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait, let me check again:.\n\nWait, let's reconstruct the digits step by step:.\n\nAfter adding each column with carries:.\n\n- Ones: 1.\n\n- Tens:8.\n\n- Hundreds:9.\n\n- Thousands:6.\n\n- Ten thousands:2 (with a carryover 1).\n\n- Hundred thousands: (9+4)=13 plus 1 carried over is 14. So 4 in the hundred thousands, carryover 1 to millions.\n\nTherefore, the digits are:.\n\nMillions place: 1 (from the carryover).\n\nHundred thousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nTens:8.\n\nOnes:1. Therefore, my final answer is: "]] 80%|########  | 402/500 [6:59:58<1:27:11, 53.39s/it]
