it is 5, becomes 4. 16 -7 =9.\n\nNext, tens place: 4 - 6. Again, can't do that. Borrow from hundreds. The hundreds digit is 8 \u2192 becomes 7. The tens becomes 14. 14-6=8.\n\nHundreds place: 7 - 2 (since the second number has 2 in hundreds place). Wait, let me write down the numbers properly:\n\nThe second number is 379,267, so when subtracting, the digits are:\n\nMinuend: 1,235,856\n\nSubtrahend:\u00a0 379,267\n\nSo aligning:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 1,2 3 5,8 5 6\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 -\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 3 7 9,2 6 7\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 --------------------\n\nStarting from the ones:\n\n6 -7: borrow, as before. So 16-7=9, tens digit becomes 4.\n\nTens: 4 -6: borrow again, so 14 -6=8, hundreds digit (originally 8) becomes 7.\n\nHundreds place:7 (after borrowing) minus 2 (from 267's hundreds place). 7-2=5.\n\nThousands place:5 (from 1,235,856's thousands place) minus 9 (from 379,267's thousands place). Wait, hold on. Let me check the thousands digit again.\n\nWait, the minuend is 1,235,856. Breaking it down:\n\nDigits are:\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundreds of thousands: 2\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:8 (but after borrowing, it's 7?)\n\nWait, maybe I need to track all digits properly. Let me write the minuend as:\n\n1 (millions)\n\n2 (hundred thousands)\n\n3 (ten thousands)\n\n5 (thousands)\n\n8 (hundreds)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n6 (ones)\n\nSubtracting:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 3 (hundred thousands)\n\n7 (ten thousands)\n\n9 (thousands)\n\n2 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n7 (ones)\n\nWait, actually, the subtrahend is 379,267, so in terms of place value:\n\nHundred thousands: 3\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:9\n\nHundreds:2\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:7\n\nSo when subtracting:\n\nStarting from the ones:\n\n6 -7: borrow, becomes 16-7=9, tens digit reduces by 1.\n\nTens place was 5, now 4. 4 -6: borrow, so becomes 14-6=8. Hundreds digit was 8, now 7.\n\nHundreds place:7 - 2 =5.\n\nThousands place:5 (from minuend) minus 9